        <strong>Deteriorating situations:</strong> Countries with the sharpest increases often face
        political instability, armed conflict, narcotics-related violence, or institutional
        breakdown. These cases require urgent international attention and support.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════ SECTION 6: LIMITATIONS & RECOMMENDATIONS ═══════ -->
<div class="section" id="data-limitations">
    <h2>6. Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</h2>

    <h3>Data Limitations</h3>

    <p>This analysis is subject to several important caveats that affect the interpretation of findings:</p>

    <p><strong>1. Unbalanced Panel Structure.</strong> The dataset is an unbalanced panel—not all countries
    report data for every year. The number of reporting countries ranges from {min_cov} ({int(min_cov_year)})
    to {max_cov} ({int(max_cov_year)}). This means that changes in the global median or mean may partly
    reflect changes in <em>which</em> countries report, not just changes in actual homicide rates.</p>

    <p><strong>2. Reporting Gaps.</strong> Many countries—particularly those affected by conflict or
    institutional weakness—have sporadic or absent reporting. Paradoxically, the countries most likely to
    have high homicide rates may be the least likely to appear in the data.</p>

    <p><strong>3. United Kingdom Caveat.</strong> UK data in this dataset refer to <strong>England and Wales
    only</strong>, as reported in the original UNODC source. Scotland and Northern Ireland are reported
    separately and should not be aggregated without adjustment.</p>

    <p><strong>4. Territories &amp; Micro-States.</strong> Some territories (e.g., Puerto Rico, Hong Kong,
    Turks and Caicos) are reported separately from their parent countries. Their small populations can produce
    volatile per-capita rates. These entries should not be double-counted with parent country totals.</p>

    <p><strong>5. Gender &amp; Age Data Availability.</strong> Sex- and age-disaggregated data are available
    for fewer country-years than aggregate data. Gender data covers {male_all['country'].nunique()} countries
    (vs. {both_all['country'].nunique()} for aggregate rates), and age-specific data is available for even
    fewer. Conclusions about demographic patterns should be interpreted accordingly.</p>

    <p><strong>6. No Imputation.</strong> Missing values have not been imputed. All statistics are computed
    only over observed data points, which means global aggregates can shift as the reporting pool changes.</p>

    <h3>Policy Recommendations</h3>

    <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>1. Prioritize violence prevention among young males.</strong> The 15–29 age group, particularly
        males, faces by far the highest homicide risk. Investments in youth employment, education, community
        programs, and targeted policing in high-risk areas are supported by the evidence.
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>2. Strengthen data collection and reporting.</strong> Significant gaps in reporting
        undermine the ability to monitor trends and target interventions. International support for
        statistical capacity-building in low-coverage countries would improve the evidence base for policy.
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>3. Focus regional strategies on the Americas and Africa.</strong> These two regions
        consistently report the highest homicide rates. Regional cooperation, information sharing,
        and coordinated approaches to transnational drivers of violence (e.g., drug trafficking,
        firearms proliferation) are warranted.
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>4. Learn from success stories.</strong> Countries that have achieved dramatic reductions
        in homicide rates provide actionable models for others. Systematic study of what worked—and
        under what conditions—should inform policy transfer and adaptation.
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>5. Address gender-based violence holistically.</strong> While males constitute the
        majority of homicide victims, female victimization is often linked to intimate partner violence
        and domestic contexts requiring different prevention strategies. A gendered approach to violence
        reduction is essential.
    </div>
</div>

