</div>

<!-- ═══════ SECTION 1: GLOBAL OVERVIEW ═══════ -->
<div class="section" id="global-overview">
    <h2>1. Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>

    <p>Over the 34-year observation window (1990–2023), the global median intentional homicide rate has exhibited a
    broadly <strong>downward trajectory</strong>. The median rate across reporting countries fell from {fmt(yearly_stats[yearly_stats['year']==1990]['median_rate'].values[0], 2)} per 100,000
    in 1990 to {fmt(yearly_stats[yearly_stats['year']==most_recent_good_year]['median_rate'].values[0], 2)} in {most_recent_good_year}, reflecting sustained—though uneven—progress
    in reducing lethal violence worldwide.</p>

    <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>Key finding:</strong> The global median homicide rate has declined across every successive decade:
        1990s&rarr;2000s ({change_90s_00s:+.1f}%), 2000s&rarr;2010s ({change_00s_10s:+.1f}%), and 2010s&rarr;2020s ({change_10s_20s:+.1f}%).
        However, the mean rate remains substantially higher than the median, indicating that a small number of
        high-rate countries skew the distribution.
    </div>

    <h3>Median &amp; Mean Homicide Rate Over Time</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="globalTrendChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Reporting Coverage Over Time</h3>
    <p>The number of countries reporting data to the UNODC has varied significantly over time, peaking at
    {max_cov} countries in {int(max_cov_year)} and dropping to {min_cov} in {int(min_cov_year)}.
    This changing composition of reporting countries means that year-over-year comparisons should be interpreted
    with caution—the observed trend reflects changes in both actual homicide rates <em>and</em> the set of countries
    that contribute data in any given year.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="coverageChart"></canvas>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════ SECTION 2: REGIONAL COMPARISON ═══════ -->
<div class="section" id="regional-comparison">
    <h2>2. Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</h2>

    <p>Regional disparities in homicide rates are stark. As of {most_recent_good_year}, the Americas and Africa
    report the highest median rates, while Europe and Asia exhibit substantially lower levels of lethal violence.
    These patterns have been broadly consistent throughout the observation period, though important shifts have
    occurred within regions.</p>

    <h3>Regional Summary ({most_recent_good_year})</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Region</th><th>Median Rate</th><th>Mean Rate</th><th>Countries Reporting</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {regional_rows}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Regional Median Trends (1990–2023)</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="regionalTrendChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Highest Homicide Rate Countries (Most Recent Year Available)</h3>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Year</th><th>Rate per 100k</th><th>Region</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{top10_rows}</tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="insight-box danger">
        <strong>Concentration of risk:</strong> The ten countries with the highest rates are overwhelmingly in the
        Americas and Africa. Small island nations and territories in the Caribbean feature prominently, where
        even a small absolute number of homicides translates to a high per-capita rate.
    </div>

    <h3>Lowest Homicide Rate Countries (Excluding Zero-Rate Micro-States)</h3>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>#</th><th>Country</th><th>Year</th><th>Rate per 100k</th><th>Region</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{bot10_rows}</tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Top 10 vs. Bottom 10 Comparison</h3>
    <div class="chart-row">
        <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="top10Chart"></canvas></div>
        <div class="chart-container"><canvas id="bot10Chart"></canvas></div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════ SECTION 3: GENDER DISPARITY ═══════ -->
<div class="section" id="sex-disparity">
    <h2>3. Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</h2>

    <p>Intentional homicide is overwhelmingly gendered. Across the dataset, <strong>males are consistently
    victimized at rates several times higher than females</strong>. In {most_recent_good_year}, the median
    male-to-female homicide rate ratio stood at <strong>{male_female_ratio:.1f}x</strong>, meaning that for
    every female homicide victim, approximately {male_female_ratio:.0f} males were killed per capita.</p>

    <div class="insight-box danger">
        <strong>Critical KPI — Male-to-Female Ratio:</strong> The {male_female_ratio:.1f}x ratio in {most_recent_good_year}
        underscores that targeted violence prevention for males—particularly young men—is essential.
        This ratio has remained broadly stable over the observation period, suggesting structural factors
        rather than episodic ones.
    </div>

    <h3>Male vs. Female Median Homicide Rates Over Time</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="genderTrendChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Male-to-Female Ratio Trend</h3>
    <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="genderRatioChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Gender Disparity by Region ({most_recent_good_year})</h3>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Region</th><th>Male Median Rate</th><th>Female Median Rate</th><th>Ratio</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{gender_reg_rows}</tbody>
    </table>

    <p>The gender gap varies substantially by region. The Americas exhibit the highest male-to-female ratio,
    driven by gang-related and interpersonal violence that disproportionately affects young men. Europe and
    Asia show somewhat lower ratios, though males remain the majority of victims in every region.</p>
</div>

<!-- ═══════ SECTION 4: AGE GROUP ═══════ -->
<div class="section" id="age-group-analysis">
    <h2>4. Age Group Analysis</h2>

    <p>Age-disaggregated data reveal a clear pattern: <strong>young adults aged 18–29 face the highest homicide
    risk</strong>, with rates declining for older age groups. Children (0–14) and the elderly (60+) experience
    the lowest rates, though any homicide in these groups is of particular policy concern.</p>

    <h3>Homicide Rate by Age Group (All Available Data)</h3>
    <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Age Group</th><th>Median Rate</th><th>Mean Rate</th><th>Observations</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>{age_rows}</tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="ageChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <h3>Age-Sex Interaction</h3>
    <p>The age-risk profile differs markedly between sexes. Males in the 18–29 age range face dramatically
    elevated rates compared to females in the same age group, reinforcing the intersection of age and gender
    as key risk factors for homicide victimization.</p>

    <div class="chart-container">
        <canvas id="ageSexChart"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="insight-box">
        <strong>Policy implication:</strong> The concentration of homicide risk among young males aged 15–29
        suggests that violence prevention programs targeting this demographic—through employment initiatives,
        conflict resolution training, and community-based interventions—are likely to yield the highest
        returns in reducing overall homicide rates.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ═══════ SECTION 5: NOTABLE TRENDS ═══════ -->
<div class="section" id="notable-trends">
    <h2>5. Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</h2>

    <p>While the global trajectory has been broadly positive, individual country experiences diverge
    dramatically. Some nations have achieved remarkable reductions in homicide rates, while others have
    seen significant deterioration. The tables below compare the average rate in each country's earliest
    available period with its most recent period (minimum 5 years of data required).</p>

    <h3>Largest Decreases in Homicide Rate</h3>
